Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/fa359c604ba4bb24805097dd63e07783f72bba9c3e2657ec6c29a3fee129e3e6.png) solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/ec6e981dc2ac04d82a79e2b13a53b8d54364d155310cbe38bf3724792b89047d.png) as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/3a6d8a97c6d07c78562c73dbd1abef1b1a3c0d9f38cd14f4f9874df091492b9e.png)" 13194139535698 true] [17592186166473 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/a3ea75c2afeb553be2bd71c6717e1fa0.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/cb8af2968caaacb5b676cea6316430ee.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n$\\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1 < 0$\nthus,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nis the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/ccc3466bc4de9ad91d2f893096827841.png)" 13194139535698 false] [17592186166475 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T,$ respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)." 13194139535698 true] [17592186166475 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 9x(t) + 6y(t); y^{′}(t) =-6x(t) - 6y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T$, respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)." 13194139535698 false] [17592186166475 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/1c9e5558e6c3c4a97881100ad5a36c5ab6b2c602eb521123371bdde9c8450a6e.png)\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a14a160428831f0fdf8a22ebcb932e655046fe4c4e258bc0d83b25c97ecfafbb.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -\\dfrac12x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/c2114a7edccbf2b296cca7e1cb9c08cd29ba063745a00d55de2ed06026aa2060.png)\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/01186c121850d622e0f4216bb6375f7ef11d2d4cef0dd2acf2eb8f99d2a16643.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/0a96347bbf236e77b719686bfe2cb275ad9a4cc1061edc1dacf3a70be423d9fc.png)\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point. \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/2710e11309547500aa3e9ba89f31281cbbc28244509046ed25a6c151c10c23ed.png)" 13194139535698 true] [17592186166475 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -\\dfrac12x$.\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$. So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-2x$.\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/0e06d98a83065e6228e2e9238e34f00e.png)" 13194139535698 false] [17592186166476 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/270b7c6ff796937603596f26e70886f41438ce87c043d3a1b4fc221398b8692f.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T,$ respectively. \n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ and $0.$" 13194139535698 true] [17592186166476 1100 "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T$, respectively.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/7f0b26ad72c30f42ceeabc3bf1096678.png)\n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$, and $0$." 13194139535698 false] [17592186166476 1145 "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/7282a63a66c9f449844292764e4e7b6f1ec472a47ff18aa7fd0c68dbe6042dbe.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/9bff79e13c06645253e57b8e6f43689c7ce21ac818c3ffdc677e03b5a9b08eb7.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown 