le}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/6337a670b6fa73c4c21759b600269cb83d808a1c13026c90e2d02519fa31cd67.png)" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361879 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361884 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361889 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361894 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361899 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361902 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361907 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361912 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361915 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1089 "q-332833" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/f1335e5420fe6e475cded1092e9b1ba621e0d88f46110b69ffef5e35ed080d6e.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a53bb4e6cd7552b92424b168d0cda90fa1085b5e72fbf8a3c37a3a948bdcf9ad.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}}  of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/ecccffb89d43b8de4ce0952d9b8d9686e74670b4d86b29687c76355b70a6ac7d.png)" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1113 #uuid "fe606e21-16dc-5275-bda3-617803b9b0bf" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361878 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approac