traight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/6a4c15275ba80d01a90b2e010ed95e95f9a75cfa13f8e51db3433f6356949057.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector \n{{field-10}} \nas they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/f838a055e525a264bf36b8e0478d186fe17753bba8c0088fea01ac7c5c1f8d7a.png)" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361949 1113 #uuid "6f3307c5-d585-5fa2-81f6-a2c996b7b9d1" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361949 1145 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/44d0f3d13ca03a0644b2f28df9200cd521b7b44d625f346e981d2a54165284f5.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/6a4c15275ba80d01a90b2e010ed95e95f9a75cfa13f8e51db3433f6356949057.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/f838a055e525a264bf36b8e0478d186fe17753bba8c0088fea01ac7c5c1f8d7a.png)" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361951 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361952 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361953 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361954 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1041 "field-1" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361954 13194139535698 true] [17592186361950 1110 #uuid "ab8e584e-d67f-511e-90a6-d46adfe6de9a" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361951 1034 "{e}^{3t}" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361951 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535698 true] [17592186361951 1111 #uuid "a335f46e-ba05-5ce6-866f-3882d14f0e87" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361952 1034 "{e}^{t}" 13194139535698 true] [17592186361952 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13